Fiqures 7.1 to
7.4
It may be seen that the trajectories denoted by Figures 7.1 to7.4 are directly related to those of Figures 3.1 to 3.4, the only dif-ference being a scale factor determined by a ratio of the f;o va'lues ofeach individual trajectory. Accordingly, the same cornments apply. Dueto RMS constra ints, tf," f,- = A.2 fps trajectories shown in Figures 7.1oto 7.4 form a one impulse rendezvous envelope for the various approachdirections. For example, a periodic (direction l, Figure 7.1) rendez-Jvous with Vo = 0.2 fps results in a X axis stationkeeping location of717 feet. A l"rg.r f, value results in a proportionally larger sta-otionkeeping distance. Thus, the maximum I imit on stationkeeping dis-tance for a one-impulse, periodic rendezvous is 717 feet.
